hitecture successfully achieves the primary goal of combining linear attention with hierarchical reasoning for computational scalability and advanced reasoning capabilities.\n- Multi-timescale processing and cross-modal fusion align well with theoretical expectations of creating synergistic interactions between fast and slow cognitive processes.\n- The dual-path System 1/System 2 model effectively mirrors human cognitive architectures, providing a solid foundation for further development.\n\n### Theoretical Gaps:\n- The observed limitations in language understanding and ambiguity resolution suggest that the current implementation may not fully capture the complexity of human linguistic processing.\n- There is a potential mismatch between theoretical expectations of hierarchical state management and practical performance on memory-intensive tasks.\n\n## Research Integration Opportunities\n\n### Addressing Observed Weaknesses:\n1. **Enhanced Cross-Modal Fusion**: Research on attention mechanisms in transformers (e.g., Vaswani et al., 2017) could inform improvements to the integration layer, enabling more nuanced information flow.\n2. **Advanced State Management**: Techniques from recurrent neural networks (RNNs) and long short-term memory (LSTM) architectures could enhance hierarchical state management for better long-range dependency modeling.\n3. **Adaptive Reasoning Mechanisms**: Cognitive psychology research on metacognition and adaptive control strategies (e.g., Dunlosky & Metcalfe, 2009) could provide insights into more sophisticated convergence detection mechanisms.\n4. **Parameter Allocation Optimization**: Research on neural architecture search (NAS) and efficient model compression techniques could optimize parameter utilization between System 1 and System 2 modules.\n\n## Innovation Opportunity Identification\n\n### Concrete Architectural Improvements:\n1. **Enhanced Cross-Modal Fusion Layer**: Incorporate more advanced attention mechanisms from transformer 